---
import DocPage from '../../components/DocPage.astro';
import Callout from '../../components/Callout.astro';
import CodeBlock from '../../components/CodeBlock.astro';
const qa = [
  'git clone https://github.com/gua-project/gua.git',
  'cd gua',
  'cmake --preset windows-qa -DGUA_BUILD_EXAMPLES=OFF',
  'cmake --build --preset windows-qa --target gua gua-runtime gua-godot',
  'pwsh -NoProfile -File scripts/stage-remote-build.ps1 -BuildDirectory build/windows-qa -OutputDirectory artifacts/qa -Flavor qa -Remote on -GodotExtension build/windows-qa/stage/addons/gua/bin/gua_godot.windows.release.x86_64.dll',
].join('\n');
const dev = [
  'cmake -S . -B build/dev-remote -G "Visual Studio 18 2026" -A x64 -DGUA_DISTRIBUTION_FLAVOR=development -DGUA_ENABLE_REMOTE_AUTOMATION=ON -DGUA_STAGE_IN_SAMPLE=OFF -DGUA_BUILD_EXAMPLES=OFF -DGUA_GODOT_CONFIGURATION=Debug',
  'cmake --build build/dev-remote --config Debug --target gua gua-runtime gua-godot',
].join('\n');
const godot = [
  'extends Control',
  'const Adapter = preload("res://addons/gua/gua_auto_adapter.gd")',
  'var ui = Adapter.new()',
  'func _ready() -> void:',
  '  ui.attach(self)',
  '  ui.update("title")',
  '  if ui.get_build_info().get("remoteEnabled", false):',
  '    if not ui.start_inspector_bridge(8765):',
  '      push_error("The compiled bridge could not start")',
  'func _process(_delta: float) -> void:',
  '  ui.update("title")',
].join('\n');
---
<DocPage eyebrow="BUILD / REMOTE AUTOMATION" title="Choose a build that can be controlled remotely" lead="For v1.2.0 and later, choose the native capability before compiling and packaging. A session profile can narrow that capability; it cannot add missing code." description="Gua v1.2.0 and later explicit remote build setting, ordinary release (no remote control), testing, development, AI-player-enabled release, Godot, Unity, native manifests, and migration from v1.1.2." section="remote-builds">
  <Callout kind="warn" title="Match the guide and integration version"><p>This build contract applies to v1.2.0 and later. When migrating from v1.1.2, keep the game add-on, native libraries, managed assemblies, and test packages at matching versions. Do not apply the v1.2.0 API examples to an old native DLL.</p><p><a href="/docs/release-1-2-0/">v1.2.0 migration note</a> · <a href="/godot/ui/">Godot installation</a> · <a href="/unity/ui/">Unity installation</a></p></Callout>
  <h2 id="three-choices">Three separate choices</h2>
  <p>First choose who will use the game and why: ordinary players, developers, testers, or an AI participant. This purpose is separate from Debug/Release, which controls debugging support and optimization. Both an ordinary release without remote control and a remotely controlled test executable can use Release configuration.</p>
  <ol><li><strong>Debug or Release</strong> controls the build configuration. Release is not proof that remote access is absent, and Debug alone does not enable it.</li><li><strong>Compiled capability</strong> is chosen with <code>GUA_DISTRIBUTION_FLAVOR</code> and <code>GUA_ENABLE_REMOTE_AUTOMATION</code> when building native Gua. It decides whether remote connections and processing of incoming operation requests exist in the artifact.</li><li><strong>Runtime access profile</strong> selects detailed inspection (Debug) or only information and actions exposed to players (Player). It controls the view and permitted operations within the compiled ceiling. <code>GUA_OBSERVATION_PROFILE=player</code> restricts an available runtime; it does not create a connection or compile in a bridge.</li></ol>
  <h2 id="matrix">Choose the artifact for your purpose</h2>
  <div class="table-wrap"><table><thead><tr><th>Purpose → <code>GUA_DISTRIBUTION_FLAVOR</code></th><th><code>GUA_ENABLE_REMOTE_AUTOMATION</code></th><th>Configuration</th><th>Remote ceiling</th></tr></thead><tbody>
    <tr><td>Ordinary user-facing release (no remote control): <code>shipping</code></td><td><code>OFF</code> required; default</td><td>Release</td><td>None: remote implementation excluded</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Development / Editor use: <code>development</code></td><td><code>OFF</code> for local use; explicit <code>ON</code> for remote use</td><td>Debug or Release</td><td>Debug when ON; host can narrow to Player</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Release test executable: <code>qa</code></td><td>Explicit <code>ON</code> required</td><td>Release</td><td>Debug; host can narrow to Player</td></tr>
    <tr><td>AI-player-enabled release: <code>ai-player</code></td><td>Explicit <code>ON</code> required</td><td>Release</td><td>Player, fixed at compile time</td></tr>
  </tbody></table></div>
  <p>A fresh native build defaults to <code>shipping</code> and <code>GUA_ENABLE_REMOTE_AUTOMATION=OFF</code>. The build rejects remote ON for an ordinary release, remote OFF for testing or an AI-player-enabled release, and non-Release configuration for any of those three purposes. The local core, observation/recording APIs, and compatible bridge function signatures are retained where needed. In an ordinary release, bridge calls return failure without running a remote implementation: the exported function name is not evidence of a usable remote implementation.</p>
  <Callout title="Two release executables with different purposes"><p>Use <code>qa</code> for a tester that needs Debug inspection of a Release executable. Use <code>ai-player</code> when the shipped game deliberately supports an AI participant. For a game that should not be controlled through Gua, distribute <code>shipping</code>. Do not ship all variants and choose one with an environment variable.</p></Callout>
  <h2 id="build">Build from the matching source</h2>
  <p>Run these commands from the Gua source checkout containing this contract. Use a source revision matching the integration version. Use Git, CMake and a C++20 toolchain. The Windows presets below require Visual Studio 18 2026 C++ tools and a CMake version supporting that generator. Godot native builds also need Python and godot-cpp dependencies; the first configure may fetch those dependencies. Staging/probing scripts require PowerShell 7 and .NET 10 SDK.</p>
  <div class="table-wrap"><table><thead><tr><th>Windows preset</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody>
    <tr><td><code>windows-shipping</code></td><td>ordinary release (no remote control) / OFF / Release</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>windows-qa</code></td><td>testing / ON / Release</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>windows-ai-player</code></td><td>AI-player-enabled release / ON / Release</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>windows-msvc-debug</code>, <code>windows-msvc-release</code></td><td>Existing development fixtures: explicitly ON, not ordinary release (no remote control)</td></tr>
  </tbody></table></div>
  <h3>Build a Godot test executable in Release configuration</h3>
  <CodeBlock title="Windows / testing / Release — source build and isolated package" lang="powershell" code={qa} />
  <p>The last command probes the actual native libraries, checks their identity, prepares the add-on, and verifies the package. The destination must be empty. Copy <code>artifacts/qa/addons/gua</code> into the game's export staging copy and export using the Release extension. This Release-only add-on does not supply the Editor/Debug extension; keep the development Editor setup separate. Do not copy only a DLL into an old add-on containing other variants.</p>
  <p>For ordinary release (no remote control), use <code>windows-shipping</code>, <code>build/windows-shipping</code>, <code>artifacts/shipping</code>, <code>-Flavor shipping -Remote off</code>, and the corresponding Release extension path. For an AI-player-enabled release, use <code>windows-ai-player</code>, <code>build/windows-ai-player</code>, <code>artifacts/ai-player</code>, and <code>-Flavor ai-player -Remote on</code>. Keep separate build and staging directories.</p>
  <h3>Enable remote access for development / Editor use</h3>
  <CodeBlock title="Isolated Debug development build" lang="powershell" code={dev} />
  <p>For a local-only development build, configure a fresh <code>build/dev-local</code> with <code>GUA_ENABLE_REMOTE_AUTOMATION=OFF</code>. For a Release development build, keep the build purpose <code>development</code> and select Release for both native and Godot configuration. When staging Debug, pass <code>-Configuration Debug -EngineBuildKind debug</code> and the Debug extension path. <code>GUA_STAGE_IN_SAMPLE</code> defaults OFF and may be ON only for development; the two legacy development presets deliberately turn it ON.</p>
  <p>For a C++/.NET-only source build, configure with <code>-DGUA_BUILD_GODOT=OFF</code>, build <code>gua</code> and <code>gua-runtime</code>, and omit <code>-GodotExtension</code> from staging. On a single-configuration generator such as Ninja, set <code>-DCMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=Release</code> for ordinary release (no remote control), testing, or AI-player-enabled release in addition to the same build purpose/remote flags. <code>GUA_BUILD_WS_BRIDGE=ON</code> alone is not an explicit remote enablement and is rejected when the main remote flag is OFF.</p>
  <h2 id="godot">Godot: select the add-on before exporting</h2>
  <p>Use the staged add-on for the selected build purpose and native configuration. Godot's Debug/Release feature selection and <code>OS.has_feature()</code> choose or inspect resources; they do not remove remote code from a prebuilt GDExtension. The desktop staging tool emits the selected library descriptor and excludes WebMCP resources and attachment code from the ordinary release.</p>
  <CodeBlock title="v1.2.0 API: keep local collection when remote is absent" lang="gdscript" code={godot} />
  <p>On desktop, <code>remoteEnabled=true</code> permits attempting the bridge; a failed start can still mean a port conflict or startup error. On Godot Web, use the supported same-page WebMCP path rather than a native WebSocket listener. The profile still cannot exceed the native ceiling.</p>
  <h2 id="unity">Unity: match native (C++) and managed (C#) compilation</h2>
  <ol><li>Build or obtain a native core/runtime pair for the chosen build purpose. Replacing only a game-side <code>#if</code> does not change a precompiled native DLL or static archive.</li><li>When compiling the Unity adapter source, define <code>GUA_REMOTE_AUTOMATION</code> for a remote-enabled adapter. Define <code>GUA_PLAYER_ONLY</code> as well for an AI-player-enabled release; it requires the first define. For ordinary release (no remote control), compile without these defines. Project defines do not rebuild a precompiled <code>Gua.Unity.dll</code>: select or rebuild the matching assembly/package.</li><li>Set <code>GUA_BUILD_VARIANT</code> in the Player build process to <code>shipping</code>, <code>development</code>, <code>qa</code>, or <code>ai-player</code>. Its default is <code>shipping</code>. It tells the prebuild guard what to verify; it does not modify native capabilities. For example, <code>$env:GUA_BUILD_VARIANT = "ai-player"</code> is only the build-intent setting after preparing the native and managed artifacts for an AI-player-enabled release.</li><li>Testing, ordinary release (no remote control), and AI-player-enabled release require a Player build with the <code>Development</code> option disabled. The guard checks compiled managed capability, selected native identity, and manifest/hash agreement before generation. Resolve a mismatch by replacing the incorrect artifact, not by renaming it.</li></ol>
  <p>The repository sample explicitly enables development automation. Its <code>GuaUnityEditorCommands.BuildPlayer</code> helper takes intent from <code>-guaVariant</code>, defaulting to <code>development</code>, and sets <code>GUA_BUILD_VARIANT</code> itself. When using that helper, pass the intended option such as <code>-guaVariant ai-player</code>; setting the environment variable alone is overwritten by the helper's default. This is not an ordinary release (no remote control) build command, and the option does not rebuild native libraries or change managed defines. Supply the matching artifacts first.</p>
  <h2 id="packages">Prepare and verify the files to distribute</h2>
  <p>Staging means collecting the files to include with the game in an empty, separate directory. A manifest records their build purpose, identity, and hashes so packaging checks can reject files mixed from different builds.</p>
  <p>A file name containing “Release” does not guarantee that remote-control code is absent. The existing development presets and SDK build workflows explicitly enable remote access. Select a verified artifact with the intended build purpose and matching integration version.</p>
  <ul><li>Use separate output directories for preparing each package for ordinary release (no remote control), testing, development, and AI-player-enabled release. Keep the native core/runtime pair, Godot extension, Unity managed assembly, and Web resources consistent.</li><li>Preserve <code>gua-build-manifest.json</code> and its native identity/hash checks. Unknown, extra, stale, or mismatched native files must fail packaging/build validation. Renaming a testing DLL does not make it safe to distribute.</li><li>Native NuGet packing requires <code>GuaNativeAssetsRoot</code> with verified manifests and binaries for <code>win-x64</code>, <code>linux-x64</code>, <code>osx-x64</code>, and <code>osx-arm64</code>. The pack properties default to <code>GuaDistributionFlavor=shipping</code> and <code>GuaEnableRemoteAutomation=false</code>; packing for testing must explicitly use <code>qa</code> and <code>true</code>. These properties validate existing binaries; they do not recompile them. The old unlabeled single-DLL fallback is refused.</li><li>Native NuGet packages carry component manifests such as <code>gua-core-build-manifest.json</code> and <code>gua-runtime-build-manifest.json</code>. Keep versions and build identity aligned across dependencies. Do not publish different variants under the same package ID/version to the same feed.</li></ul>
  <h3 id="web">Web compilation is also explicit</h3>
  <p>Activate the matching Emscripten toolchain first: <code>emcmake</code> and <code>node</code> must be on PATH. The source helper <code>scripts/build-web-native.ps1</code> accepts <code>-Flavor</code> and <code>-Remote</code>; its legacy defaults are <code>development</code> and <code>on</code>, with Debug configuration. Ordinary release, testing, and AI-player-enabled builds require <code>-Configuration Release</code>. For example, <code>pwsh -File scripts/build-web-native.ps1 -RuntimeOnly -Configuration Release -Flavor ai-player -Remote on -BuildDirectory build/web-ai-player</code> builds Unity static archives and checks their linked identity under Node. Use a separate directory and <code>-Flavor shipping -Remote off</code> for local-only ordinary release (no remote control) compilation.</p>
  <p>This helper's native/WASM outputs are not by themselves a verified final Godot Web or Unity WebGL package. Complete the engine-specific resource/assembly selection and export validation too. An ordinary release (no remote control) must omit the WebMCP implementation and entry points; a runtime check around attachment is not code removal. The desktop <code>stage-remote-build.ps1</code> probe is not a WASM package verifier. See <a href="/webmcp/">WebMCP</a> for same-page operation and supported browser features.</p>
  <h2 id="inspect">Check the compiled capability first</h2>
  <p>Read immutable build-info using <code>gua_copy_build_info_json</code> / <code>gua_runtime_copy_build_info_json</code> in C/C++, <code>Gua.Core.GuaBuildInfo.GetJson()</code> / <code>GuaRuntime.GetBuildInfoJson()</code> in .NET, or <code>ui.get_build_info()</code> / native context <code>get_build_info_json()</code> in Godot. Check <code>flavor</code>, <code>configuration</code>, <code>remoteEnabled</code>, <code>remotePermissionCeiling</code>, <code>buildId</code>, <code>sourceCommit</code>, and <code>transports</code>.</p>
  <p>An ordinary release (no remote control) cannot recover the absent implementation through environment variables, startup arguments, or bridge APIs. An AI-player-enabled release starts in Player and rejects Debug elevation through APIs, reset/reconnect, client factories, and borrowed/unscoped consumer paths. Existing <a href="/agent-policy/">Player exposure rules</a> and separate <a href="/game-input/">game-input permissions</a> still apply at enqueue and host consumption. A build with the Player access profile does not automatically grant keyboard, pointer, or gameplay-action access.</p>
  <p>If <code>remoteEnabled=false</code>, inability to connect is expected; changing the port or setting the Player profile cannot enable it. If it is true, check startup, profile/transport support, and port use. A missing listener alone does not prove that code was removed: keep package identity, compile/link inspection, and positive/negative tests in the distribution pipeline. For example, the Gua source provides <code>verify-remote-build.ps1</code> and <code>verify-remote-build-graph.ps1</code> (Windows/MSVC).</p>
  <h2 id="migrate">Migrate an existing v1.1.2 project</h2>
  <ol class="steps"><li><strong>Choose the intended use.</strong><p>Normal shipped game → ordinary release (no remote control); automated Debug inspection of a Release executable → testing; Editor testing with remote access enabled → development; AI participant in a shipped game → AI-player-enabled release.</p></li><li><strong>Replace the complete integration.</strong><p>Use matching-version native, managed, add-on, and test components. Remove stale copies from old bin/plugin directories; do not keep the old testing DLL as a fallback.</p></li><li><strong>Configure a fresh build directory.</strong><p>Set the build purpose and explicit remote flag together. A cached old WS option or a Release export preset is not the new explicit remote enablement.</p></li><li><strong>Recompile the layers you own.</strong><p>Build native Gua with the selected capability. For Unity source adapters, match the managed defines; for precompiled adapters, obtain the matching build. Keep Player policy as a separate host decision.</p></li><li><strong>Stage, verify, then export.</strong><p>Use an empty destination, preserve manifests, and reject mismatches. Verify that ordinary release (no remote control) cannot start a bridge, an AI-player-enabled release cannot gain Debug and can perform only allowed operations, and testing/development remote operations still work when enabled.</p></li><li><strong>Update CI and test expectations.</strong><p>Install the selected variant rather than blindly taking a development SDK. A remote test cannot operate an ordinary release (no remote control) artifact; test that absence separately and run interactive scenarios against testing or permitted AI-player-enabled release artifacts.</p></li></ol>
  <p>Authoritative source: <a href="https://github.com/gua-project/gua/blob/2e12fb3cfdf822418f349515027d6f620c5ee63b/cmake/GuaRemoteBuild.cmake">native flags and validation</a> · <a href="https://github.com/gua-project/gua/blob/2e12fb3cfdf822418f349515027d6f620c5ee63b/CMakePresets.json">presets</a> · <a href="https://github.com/gua-project/gua/blob/2e12fb3cfdf822418f349515027d6f620c5ee63b/protocol/specs/remote-build-variants.md">build contract</a> · <a href="https://github.com/gua-project/gua/blob/2e12fb3cfdf822418f349515027d6f620c5ee63b/scripts/stage-remote-build.ps1">desktop staging</a> · <a href="https://github.com/gua-project/gua/blob/2e12fb3cfdf822418f349515027d6f620c5ee63b/bindings/unity/Editor/GuaVariantBuildGuard.cs">Unity build guard</a></p>
</DocPage>
